<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/5th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Foedus Cassianum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 493 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Foedus Cassianum was a treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> League, traditionally dated to 493 BCE and named after the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spurius-cassius-vecellinus">Spurius Cassius</a>. Concluded after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-regillus">Battle of Lake Regillus</a>, it established peace and a defensive alliance in which both sides shared command and booty. It governed Roman–Latin relations until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-war">Latin War</a> of 340–338 BCE.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In their first treaty with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> listed the surrounding countryside as their own territory, a claim the Latin League contested. The war that followed ended in a Roman victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-regillus">Battle of Lake Regillus</a> and a conditional surrender soon after. According to Roman tradition the treaty was then made between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and thirty Latin cities as two independent powers, placing Rome as equal in power to all the members of the league combined. It took its name from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spurius-cassius-vecellinus">Spurius Cassius Vecellinus</a>, consul at the time, who ratified it in Rome on Rome's behalf.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Besides peace, the treaty joined the Roman and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> armies for mutual defence against the Italic tribes, divided all spoils of war, provided for joint colonies on captured land, and set up a community of private rights between the citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and of any Latin city. A bronze copy survived in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-forum">Roman Forum</a> until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>'s day, but the original is lost.</p><p>The version given by Dionysius of Halicarnassus calls for peace as long as the heavens and the earth remain where they are. Neither side was to make war on the other, bring in foreign enemies or grant them passage; each was to help the other with all its forces and take an equal share of booty from common wars. Suits on private contracts were to be settled within ten days, in the nation where the contract was made, and nothing could be changed without the consent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> and of all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 486 BCE Rome, again through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spurius-cassius-vecellinus">Spurius Cassius Vecellinus</a>, made a treaty with the Hernici on apparently similar terms, and the Hernici may have been admitted to the Foedus Cassianum itself, though this is not certain. By adding the military power of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> to that of the young republic, the treaty allowed Rome to expand across much of the Italian Peninsula.</p><p>It was later regarded as the beginning of the network of socii, Roman protectorates that became a major factor in the Republic's rise, and it was a model for later treaties with other Italic peoples. The treaty was renewed in 358 BCE, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> reneged on it soon after; once Rome had overcome the league's other members in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-war">Latin War</a>, the treaty was void.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Foedus Cassianum” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
